"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][22]={"university_biology_en:M22-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M22-L1 – Molecular evolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Evolutionary Genomics and Biodiversity<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a>: mutation, selection\nand homology.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish substitution rates,\ninterpret dN/dS and evaluate the assumptions of molecular evolutionary\nmodels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Two homologous proteins may have different amino acid sequences\ndespite shared ancestry. Molecular evolution studies which changes\npersist across generations and which processes explain the observed\npattern. Observed divergence is not directly the mutation rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"from-mutations-to-substitutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From mutations to\nsubstitutions<\/h3>\n<p>A mutation produces a new variant. In an idealised population model,\na substitution denotes the fixation of a different state along a\nlineage. Neutral variants can become fixed by drift. In the simple\nneutral diploid model, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2N\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnew mutations arise per generation at the site considered, with\nindividual fixation probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2N)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir product gives the neutral substitution rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis relationship assumes neutral mutations and a suitable idealised\npopulation, among other conditions; it does not apply to every observed\nvariant.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"coding-sequences-and-selection-models\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coding sequences and\nselection models<\/h3>\n<p>Synonymous substitutions leave the encoded amino acid unchanged;\nnonsynonymous substitutions change it. In a codon model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare estimated substitutions per available nonsynonymous and synonymous\nsite respectively. Simply dividing raw counts is therefore\ninappropriate. The ratio is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\frac{d_N}{d_S}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under a suitable model for diverged lineages,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often evidence consistent with purifying selection,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith positive selection, and a value near 1 with neutrality. A gene-wide\nmean can conceal individual adaptively evolving sites. Synonymous\nchanges can also be subject to selection. Very small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmakes the ratio unstable; it is undefined when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Codon-based comparisons require reliable orthologous sequences,\ncorrect alignment and a plausible phylogeny. Models may allow rates to\nvary among branches or sites. A likelihood comparison of a restricted\nand alternative model requires a reference distribution appropriate to\nthe question; model assumptions and multiple testing matter. Within\npopulations, segregating variants are not independent fixed\nsubstitutions. The usual interpretation of the ratio cannot simply be\ntransferred.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_115_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three model genes with dN/dS of 0.2, 1 and 1.5; the reference line is at 1.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three model genes with dN/dS of 0.2, 1\nand 1.5; the reference line is at 1.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three model genes with dN/dS of 0.2, 1 and 1.5; the\nreference line is at 1.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a fictional comparison of three plant genes, estimated rates are\nA:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_N=0.02,d_S=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nB:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10,0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nC:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.15<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.15,0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRatios are 0.2, 1 and 1.5. C warrants further investigation, but without\nuncertainty, alignment checks and model comparison it does not establish\nadaptive evolution. A may contain individual sites with different\ndynamics despite a low overall ratio.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_N=0.04,d_S=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the interpretation change when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a short sequence with several segregating variants not\nequivalent to diverged orthologous genes?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04/0.20=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The ratio is undefined; estimability of the rates must be examined\nseparately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Polymorphisms can still be lost or fixed. Their frequencies also\ndepend on demography and sampling; the substitution interpretation does\nnot apply directly.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All numbers are model data. Saturation, recombination, paralogous\ngenes, codon usage and erroneous assignments can distort results. A\nratio is not a direct measure of organismal fitness.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Molecular evolutionary models connect sequence change with explicit\nassumptions. The next lesson examines variation among populations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2596312/\">Kryazhimskiy\n&amp; Plotkin (2008) – The Population Genetics of dN/dS<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nInterpretation across evolutionary timescales; indexed abstract and text\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M22-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M22-L2 – Population genomics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Evolutionary Genomics and Biodiversity<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L1<\/a>: allele frequencies,\ndrift and genomic data.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Quantify genetic differentiation\nand compare alternative explanations for unusual genomic regions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Populations of a species often differ in allele frequencies.\nPopulation genomics studies this variation across many genomic\npositions. It combines sampling design, sequence quality, statistical\nmodels and evolutionary hypotheses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"heterozygosity-and-differentiation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Heterozygosity and\ndifferentiation<\/h3>\n<p>For a biallelic locus with allele frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexpected heterozygosity under random mating is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=2p(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor two equally weighted populations,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the mean of their expected heterozygosities and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis heterozygosity calculated from their shared mean allele frequency. A\nsimplified differentiation measure is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{ST}=\\frac{H_T-H_S}{H_T},\\qquad H_T&gt;0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The example describes population parameters under simplified\nconditions, not a generally unbiased sample estimator. Real analyses\naccount for different sample sizes and estimation uncertainty, for\nexample with suitable F-statistic estimators. A high value can suggest\nrestricted gene flow, drift or selection but cannot distinguish these\ncauses alone. A low value does not establish complete mixing.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"genome-wide-patterns-and-hypothesis-tests\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Genome-wide patterns\nand hypothesis tests<\/h3>\n<p>Nucleotide diversity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the mean pairwise sequence difference per comparable site.\nRecombination and linkage influence the independence of genomic markers.\nSelection hypotheses are tested against demographic null models;\nbottlenecks, expansion and heterogeneous recombination can also create\nunusual regions. Differentiation, diversity and linkage patterns convey\ndifferent information. Principal component analysis may visualise\nstructure but does not explain its causes. Sampling should be justified\ngeographically and biologically rather than restricted to accessible\nindividuals.<\/p>\n<p>A robust analysis documents reference bias, missing genotypes,\nfiltering criteria, relatedness and uncertainty. Genomic windows may be\njointly affected by linkage. An extreme value chosen after analysis is\nparticularly prone to overestimation; independent populations and\npredefined comparison rules strengthen the evidence.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_116_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two equally weighted populations with frequencies 0.2 and 0.8 and the resulting differentiation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two equally weighted populations with\nfrequencies 0.2 and 0.8 and the resulting differentiation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two equally weighted populations with frequencies 0.2 and\n0.8 and the resulting differentiation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Two fictional populations have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_1=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_2=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=0.32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_S=0.32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean frequency is 0.5 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_T=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe simplified measure is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.32<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.5-0.32)/0.5=0.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt another locus with identical frequencies of 0.5, it is zero. A\ndifferentiated region with low\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay reflect selection, but also linked processes or demographic\nhistory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the simplified measure for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_1=0.4,p_2=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith equal weighting.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How should a high value at one locus be interpreted?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are a thousand linked markers not a thousand independent\ntests?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.48<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_S=0.48,H_T=0.5,F_{ST}=0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">As evidence of differentiation that must be compared with\ndemography, technical quality and other markers.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Shared inheritance creates statistical dependence; the effective\nnumber of independent observations is lower.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional frequencies; the formula illustrates the heterozygosity\napproach. It does not replace an estimator appropriate to the dataset.\nGenetic structure does not establish a fixed boundary between\nspecies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Genome-wide differentiation becomes interpretable through demographic\nand statistical comparisons. M22-L3 shifts to the level of entire\nlineages.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4687486/\">Holsinger &amp;\nWeir (2009) – Genetics in geographically structured populations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinition and interpretation of F-statistics; indexed text · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3016716/\">Population\ngenomics to detect selection in natural populations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Genomic\ndifferentiation, diversity and methodological caveats; indexed text ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M22-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M22-L3 – Macroevolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Evolutionary Genomics and Biodiversity<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>: speciation,\nphylogenies and exponential models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish speciation and\nextinction rates in birth-death models and recognise limitations of\nmacroevolutionary inference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Macroevolution examines patterns at and above species level over long\nperiods. Species richness arises through branching and is reduced by\nextinction; morphological trait changes are a separate dimension. A\nspecies-rich clade need not have an exceptionally high speciation\nrate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"a-stochastic-diversification-model\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A stochastic diversification\nmodel<\/h3>\n<p>In a homogeneous birth-death model, each existing lineage produces\nnew lineages at rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile lineages become extinct at rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith constant rates, expected lineage number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[N(t)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dE[N]}{dt}=(\\lambda-\\mu)E[N],\\qquad E[N(t)]=N_0e^{(\\lambda-\\mu)t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Net diversification rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\lambda-\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of inverse time. The expectation over many model trajectories\nis not the actual count in a single trajectory; a small clade may become\nentirely extinct. Identical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan occur with high or low turnover.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"phylogenies-fossils-and-identifiability\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Phylogenies, fossils\nand identifiability<\/h3>\n<p>Dated trees of living species depict reconstructed branching of\nsurviving lineages. Extinct branches without living descendants are\nmissing. With time-varying rates, very different speciation and\nextinction histories can be consistent with the same observed patterns.\nIdentifiability means different parameters can in principle be\ndistinguished with ideal data; more data of the same type do not\nautomatically resolve structural non-identifiability. Fossils can\nprovide additional information but are affected by preservation,\nsampling effort, dating and taxonomic assignment.<\/p>\n<p>Adaptive radiation connects diversification with ecological and\nphenotypic differentiation. Temporal coincidence of trait change and\nincreasing species counts does not establish causation. Comparative\nanalyses must consider shared ancestry, alternative environmental\nfactors and observation processes. Mass extinctions can alter lineage\ncomposition; subsequent radiations do not necessarily restore the same\ndiversity.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_117_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two birth-death scenarios with equal expected lineage counts but different individual rates.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two birth-death scenarios with equal\nexpected lineage counts but different individual rates.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two birth-death scenarios with equal expected lineage\ncounts but different individual rates.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A model starts with ten lineages. Scenario A:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper million years; B:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand after ten million years\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>27.18<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[N]=10e^1\\approx27.18<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA has higher lineage turnover. Equal expectations do not imply identical\ndistributions or fossil histories.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[N]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter five million years for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0=20,r=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper million years.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional information might distinguish the example\nscenarios?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is species richness alone not evidence of adaptive\nradiation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>54.37<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20e^1\\approx54.37<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Dated fossil occurrences, information on extinct lineages and an\nexplicit fossil detection model may help.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Richness also depends on age and extinction; ecological and\nphenotypic differentiation must be demonstrated separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The model ignores density dependence, time-varying environments and\ndifferences among lineages, among other factors. All rates are\nfictional. Equal means illustrate a teaching example, not general\nequivalence of complete probability models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Macroevolutionary reconstructions combine mathematical models with\nindependent evidence. M22-L4 considers spatial history within\nspecies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2176-1\">Louca\n&amp; Pennell (2020) – Extant timetrees are consistent with a myriad of\ndiversification histories<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Identifiability of diversification\nhistories and importance of fossils; abstract · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.ecolsys.37.091305.110035\">Birth-Death\nModels in Macroevolution<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Speciation and extinction models, fossils\nand phylogenies; abstract · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M22-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M22-L4 – Phylogeography<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Evolutionary Genomics and Biodiversity<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L2<\/a>: genealogies and\ngenetic structure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate geographically\ndistributed haplotypes, formulate historical hypotheses and assess their\nidentifiability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Populations in two mountain regions may carry similar or different\ngenetic variants. Phylogeography asks how dispersal, isolation, climate\nand demographic history shaped spatial patterns. The present\ndistribution of a variant is not necessarily its place of origin.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"genealogy-and-geography\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Genealogy and geography<\/h3>\n<p>A haplotype is a combination of variants on a jointly inherited\nsequence segment. A haplotype network connects observed or reconstructed\nsequence states through possible changes. Edges may represent a putative\nsubstitution, for example. Networks are especially useful when short\nsequence distances allow several equally plausible connections. Without\na suitable outgroup or other information, a network need not be\ndirected. A frequent haplotype need not be the oldest.<\/p>\n<p>Haplotype diversity in a sample of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindividuals with observed frequencies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often estimated as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=\\frac{n}{n-1}\\left(1-\\sum_i f_i^2\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It equals the probability of observing different haplotypes in two\nindividuals drawn without replacement. A haploid marker region is\nassumed to provide one state per individual. Unlike nucleotide\ndiversity, it does not account for how many bases differ between two\nhaplotypes.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"historical-models-and-coalescence\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Historical models and\ncoalescence<\/h3>\n<p>Coalescence describes common ancestors of gene copies backwards in\ntime. Gene trees may differ from population history because of random\nancestry, introgression or recombination. A single mitochondrial or\nplastid region may have a different history from the nuclear genome.\nModels of refugia, subsequent expansion or sustained isolation should\ntherefore be evaluated with multiple independent data sources.\nHistorical climate data, geology and spatial sampling complement\nsequences. Converting genetic distances to times depends on calibration,\nrate models and uncertainty. Comparative phylogeography tests whether\nmultiple species support a similar spatial history rather than\ngeneralising from one pattern.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_118_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Undirected H1–H2–H3 network and regional frequencies: West 8/2/0, East 1/3/6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Undirected H1–H2–H3 network and regional\nfrequencies: West 8/2/0, East 1/3/6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Undirected H1–H2–H3 network and regional frequencies:\nWest 8/2/0, East 1/3/6.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Ten haploid markers are recorded in each of two fictional regions.\nWest: H1 eight times, H2 twice; East: H1 once, H2 three times, H3 six\ntimes. For West,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.356<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(10/9)(1-0.8^2-0.2^2)\\approx0.356<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor East,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.6<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(10/9)(1-0.1^2-0.3^2-0.6^2)=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nH1 and H2 differ at one position, as do H2 and H3; the two differences\ninvolve distinct positions. Higher eastern diversity is compatible with\nseveral histories, such as different demography or unequal sampling. It\ndoes not establish an eastern origin.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor ten individuals with five H1 and five H2.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a network edge not demonstrate a direction of\nmigration?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two additional data sources for a historical refugium\nhypothesis.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.556<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(10/9)(1-0.25-0.25)=5/9\\approx0.556<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It represents sequence differences, not directly a geographic route\nor its temporal orientation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, independent nuclear markers and palaeoclimatic habitat\nmodels; fossils or dated distribution records may also be relevant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All data are simulated. Small and spatially biased samples influence\nfrequencies. The network is a simplified undirected representation, not\na reconstructed migration route.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Phylogeographic hypotheses connect genetic variants, space and\nhistory. The next lesson addresses the translation of biological\nobservations into biodiversity indicators.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC544936/\">The\nstructure of biodiversity – insights from molecular phylogeography<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nGeographic genetic structure, haplotype networks and demographic\nhistory; indexed text · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4687486/\">Holsinger &amp;\nWeir (2009) – Genetics in geographically structured populations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinition and interpretation of F-statistics; indexed text · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M22-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M22-L5 – Biodiversity indicators<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Evolutionary Genomics and Biodiversity<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L2<\/a>: monitoring,\ndiversity and genetic variation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate biodiversity\nindicators from defined baselines and evaluate their meaning for\nresearch and conservation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Changes in species richness capture only one dimension of\nbiodiversity. Populations may decline, lose genetic variation or shift\ntheir distributions even when local recorded species counts remain\nunchanged. Indicators summarise observations for a particular question;\nthey are not a complete representation of nature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"observation-variable-and-indicator\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observation, variable and\nindicator<\/h3>\n<p>A monitoring project defines taxa, spatial units, sampling times and\nmeasurements. Observations can be used to derive comparable quantities\nsuch as estimated abundance, distribution or genetic diversity. The\nEssential Biodiversity Variables (EBV) approach connects such\nstandardised quantities to further analysis. The GEO BON data portal,\nfor example, includes spatially resolved changes in species richness.\nDifferent baselines, grid sizes and taxonomic groups can nevertheless\nyield different findings.<\/p>\n<p>For a positive baseline\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrelative change is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_X=100\\frac{X_t-X_0}{X_0}\\ \\%.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For multiple populations a simplified equally weighted geometric\nindex of relative abundances can be calculated:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/munderover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mfrac><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_t=\\exp\\left(\\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\ln\\frac{N_{i,t}}{N_{i,0}}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>All ratios must be positive. A value of 1 corresponds to the\nreference level; it does not mean every population is unchanged. An\nactual index methodology must define weighting, missing values, zeros\nand uncertainty. A geometric index is not equivalent to the change in\ntotal individual counts.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"interpretation-and-decisions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interpretation and decisions<\/h3>\n<p>Detection probability, spatial coverage and changing identification\nstandards influence trends. Changes in recording should be distinguished\nfrom biological change where possible. Confidence intervals or suitable\nhierarchical models help represent uncertainty in observations and\npopulations. Conservation decisions combine indicators with specific\nobjectives, such as preserving genetic diversity or restoring habitat.\nAn index can conceal trade-offs; individual trends and data gaps should\nremain visible. For rare species, publishing precise occurrence\nlocations should be assessed for possible conservation risks.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_119_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three relative abundances 0.5, 1 and 1.28 yield a geometric index of 0.862.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three relative abundances 0.5, 1 and 1.28\nyield a geometric index of 0.862.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three relative abundances 0.5, 1 and 1.28 yield a\ngeometric index of 0.862.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Three fictional populations have relative abundances of 0.5, 1 and\n1.28 compared with their baselines. The geometric index is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.28<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.862<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.5\\cdot1\\cdot1.28)^{1/3}\\approx0.862<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na change of approximately −13.8% from 1. The arithmetic mean is\napproximately 0.927. These statistics answer different questions. In a\nseparate grid cell, observed richness falls from 40 to 36:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_X=-10\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNone of these calculations automatically corrects detection errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the geometric index for two populations with relative\nabundances of 0.5 and 2.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should an undetected taxon not automatically be assigned zero\nabundance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What information is needed to compare two surveys?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.5\\cdot2)^{1/2}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe relative increase of the second offsets the decline of the first in\nthe index.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Imperfect detection may produce zero observations even when\nindividuals are present.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Among other things, taxa, spatial unit, period, sampling effort,\ndetection method, baseline and uncertainty.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation is a simplified teaching index, not an implementation\nof an official global indicator. Zeros require explicit statistical\ntreatment; arbitrary pseudovalues can change trends. All numbers are\nfictional.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This elective connects genetic, historical and ecological evidence.\nFurther master’s elective profiles are alternative specialisations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://portal.geobon.org/ebv-detail?id=31&amp;v=1\">GEO BON\n– EBV Data Portal: species richness change<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Example of a spatially\ndefined biodiversity change variable; dataset metadata · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
